========================================================================
FAIRNESS 6 objection(s) · 1 serious
========================================================================

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The MONALEESA-2 PFS hazard ratio cited as '0.57' does not correspond to the primary analysis figure; it is the updated analysis figure, and the draft does not say which analysis it is drawing from in the newsletter body itself.
     quote         : “Progression-free survival hazard ratios of 0.54, 0.57 and 0.58, each from the analysis named on the page.”
     why           : The primary MONALEESA-2 PFS HR was 0.556 (first interim) or 0.568 (updated); the figure 0.57 appears in the NEJM OS paper as a rounded citation of the updated analysis. The newsletter defers to 'the analysis named on the page' but the newsletter itself does not name it, so a reader of the newsletter alone cannot verify which analysis produced 0.57 or confirm it is comparable to the 0.54 and 0.58 figures from the other trials. The cross-reference to the full assessment is doing work the newsletter body should do itself.
     fix           : Name the specific analysis (e.g., 'updated PFS analysis, HR 0.568, rounded to 0.57') in the newsletter body, or round all three to two decimal places from the same class of analysis and state which class.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · FACT] The draft attributes the argument about MONARCH 3's interval width to Tanguy et al. in a way that implies the paper specifically addressed abemaciclib's CI width, when the paper's actual finding was that MONARCH 3 was less powered than the other two trials — meaning a significant OS result was less likely in MONARCH 3, not that its CI width was a designed consequence.
     quote         : “the width of abemaciclib's interval is itself a consequence of what that trial was powered to detect — a point Tanguy and colleagues made formally in npj Breast Cancer in 2018”
     why           : The Tanguy 2018 paper calculated that MONARCH 3 had less statistical power to detect an OS benefit than PALOMA-2 or MONALEESA-2, because MONARCH 3 had a smaller sample and fewer planned events. The paper's conclusion was that MONARCH 3 was more likely to miss a true OS benefit — i.e., it was underpowered relative to the other trials. The draft's phrasing 'a consequence of what that trial was powered to detect' implies the CI width follows from a deliberate power calculation for OS, when in fact MONARCH 3 was powered for PFS and the Tanguy paper was an external post-hoc power analysis showing MONARCH 3 was underpowered for OS. The citation is directionally consistent with the draft's argument but mischaracterises what Tanguy et al. specifically 'made formally': they showed MONARCH 3 was less likely to reach OS significance, not that its CI width was a designed feature. A reader checking the citation will find the paper says the opposite of what 'powered to detect' implies — MONARCH 3 was powered to detect less.
     fix           : Replace 'a consequence of what that trial was powered to detect' with 'a consequence of MONARCH 3 having fewer planned events than the other two trials — a point Tanguy and colleagues made formally in npj Breast Cancer in 2018, showing MONARCH 3 had less power to detect an OS benefit than PALOMA-2 or MONALEESA-2.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft presents the PALOMA-2 OS hazard ratio as '0.96 on an interval from 0.78 to 1.18' without noting that the primary OS analysis was complicated by a significant imbalance in missing survival data between arms (21% vs 13%), which the trial investigators themselves flagged as limiting the interpretation of that figure.
     quote         : “A hazard ratio of 0.96 on an interval from 0.78 to 1.18 is not a near miss.”
     why           : The primary PALOMA-2 OS analysis had 21% of placebo-arm patients with unknown survival status versus 13% in the palbociclib arm — an imbalance the investigators flagged as limiting interpretation. A sensitivity analysis excluding those patients gave HR 0.869 (0.706–1.069), meaningfully different from 0.96. The draft uses 0.96 to argue palbociclib's result 'is not a near miss,' but the more complete sensitivity analysis gives a result closer to abemaciclib's. The draft is entitled to use the primary analysis figure, but using it without noting the data-quality caveat — when that caveat was prominent in the original publication — makes the contrast with abemaciclib look sharper than the data fully support.
     fix           : Add a parenthetical noting that the primary analysis was subject to a missing-data imbalance and that a sensitivity analysis gave HR 0.869, so the 0.96 figure should be read with that caveat.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft states the MONARCH 3 PFS hazard ratio is '0.54' without specifying whether this is the interim or final analysis figure; the interim was 0.543 and the final was 0.540, but the draft's claim that all three PFS HRs come from 'the analysis named on the page' is not verifiable from the newsletter alone.
     quote         : “Progression-free survival hazard ratios of 0.54, 0.57 and 0.58, each from the analysis named on the page.”
     why           : MONARCH 3 has two investigator-assessed PFS HRs in the literature: 0.543 (interim) and 0.540 (final). Both round to 0.54, so the figure is not wrong, but the newsletter defers to 'the analysis named on the page' for all three drugs without naming any of them in the newsletter itself. A reader who checks the figures will find multiple analyses for each trial and cannot determine from the newsletter which one was used or whether they are comparable (e.g., primary vs. updated).
     fix           : Either name the specific analysis for each drug in the newsletter body, or add a footnote stating 'all three figures are from the primary/final investigator-assessed PFS analysis for each trial.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says 'abemaciclib carries a labelled thrombosis warning' without specifying the type or severity of thrombosis events, which could overstate the clinical significance of the warning relative to how it appears on the label.
     quote         : “Abemaciclib carries a labelled thrombosis warning and causes diarrhoea in 81 to 90 percent of patients.”
     why           : The abemaciclib label includes a warning for venous thromboembolic events (VTE). Describing this simply as a 'thrombosis warning' without specifying VTE, or noting the incidence rate from the label, gives the warning more weight than a reader can assess. The diarrhoea figure (81–90%) is attributed to the label and is verifiable; the thrombosis warning is real but the bare word 'thrombosis' carries more alarm than 'VTE warning with stated incidence.' The draft applies this characterisation to abemaciclib but does not apply equivalent specificity to the ribociclib cardiac monitoring requirement or the palbociclib neutropenia figure, creating an asymmetry in how the three burdens are described.
     fix           : Specify 'venous thromboembolic events (VTE)' and add the label incidence figure, matching the level of specificity used for palbociclib's neutropenia (66%, grade 3+).

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says 'the assessment examines four' comparative studies and then says 'an outside reviewer found two more that we had missed,' but does not say whether those two additional studies were examined and incorporated or merely acknowledged — leaving the reader uncertain whether the assessment is complete.
     quote         : “Four studies are not the whole literature, and the assessment says so: an outside reviewer found two more that we had missed.”
     why           : The sentence acknowledges the gap but does not close it. A reader does not know whether the two additional studies were reviewed and found to change or not change the conclusions, or whether they were simply noted as existing. If they were reviewed and found consistent with the four, saying so would strengthen the piece. If they were not reviewed, the acknowledgement of their existence without their content is incomplete disclosure.
     fix           : Add one sentence stating whether the two additional studies were reviewed after the reviewer flagged them, and if so, whether they materially changed the conclusions.

