========================================================================
COVERAGE 6 careful treatment(s) elsewhere · 3 contradiction(s), 2 serious
========================================================================

  Scientific Reports (Kappel et al., 2024) — 'Comparative overall survival of CDK4/6 inhibitors in combination with endocrine therapy in advanced breast cancer'
     got right     : Correctly identified that individual trials show differing statistical significance for OS despite similar PFS HRs (0.50–0.59 across trials); performed a 7-trial, 4415-patient network meta-analysis and found no statistically significant OS differences between any of the three CDK4/6 inhibitors; explicitly noted that ribociclib and abemaciclib both showed significantly higher GI toxicity than palbociclib; found treatment discontinuation and death due to AEs were significantly higher with abemaciclib than with palbociclib or ribociclib; correctly attributed all figures to their source trials.
     still omitted : Does not compute the degree of CI overlap between the MONARCH 3 and MONALEESA-2 OS intervals, nor frame the grading question as one about threshold-crossing versus evidence about the drug. Does not discuss the cardiac monitoring burden specific to ribociclib as a countervailing factor to its guideline advantage.
     https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53151-8

  ESMO Open (Flatiron Health real-world study, Jan 2025) — 'Comparative overall survival of CDK4/6 inhibitors plus an aromatase inhibitor in HR+/HER2− metastatic breast cancer in the US real-world setting'
     got right     : Used stabilized inverse probability of treatment weighting (sIPTW) on 9,146 patients; found no significant OS differences between ribociclib, abemaciclib, and palbociclib plus AI (ribociclib vs. palbociclib aHR 0.98 [0.87–1.10]; abemaciclib vs. palbociclib aHR 0.95 [0.84–1.08]; abemaciclib vs. ribociclib aHR 0.97 [0.82–1.14]); explicitly excluded clinical trial participants to avoid selection bias; noted findings should inform treatment selection alongside safety and quality of life.
     still omitted : Does not discuss the CI-overlap argument between MONARCH 3 and MONALEESA-2 or the threshold-crossing framing. Does not address the cardiac monitoring burden of ribociclib. Does not note that the real-world study's own presenter (Wisinski at SABCS 2024) described abemaciclib as showing 'clinically meaningful benefit' on OS even without statistical significance — a framing that partially endorses the guideline's direction.
     https://www.esmoopen.com/article/S2059-7029(24)01874-X/fulltext

  Annals of Oncology — Final OS results of MONARCH 3 (Goetz et al., 2024)
     got right     : Correctly reported the final OS HR as 0.804 (95% CI 0.637–1.015; P=0.0664, non-significant) after 8.1 years median follow-up; explicitly stated OS was a 'gated secondary endpoint' and that 'no power assumptions were made for the secondary endpoint of OS'; reported median OS of 66.8 vs. 53.7 months (13.1-month numerical difference); did not overstate the result.
     still omitted : Does not compare the MONARCH 3 CI to the MONALEESA-2 CI or quantify their overlap. Does not discuss whether the threshold-crossing difference between the two trials is a meaningful basis for differential guideline grading.
     https://www.annalsofoncology.org/article/S0923-7534(24)00139-X/fulltext

  OncoDaily — 'NCCN Breast Cancer Version 4.2026: Metastatic Treatment Moves Further Toward Biomarker-Driven Sequencing'
     got right     : Correctly reported that ribociclib plus AI is Category 1 and abemaciclib/palbociclib plus AI remain Category 2A; explicitly stated the guideline's own language that 'CDK4/6 inhibitors have not been directly compared in randomized trials'; noted treatment selection should consider 'efficacy, toxicity, comorbidities, monitoring requirements, and patient preference' — acknowledging monitoring burden as a selection factor.
     still omitted : Does not discuss the CI-overlap between MONARCH 3 and MONALEESA-2, does not quantify how close abemaciclib's OS result came to significance, and does not engage with the network meta-analysis or real-world comparative data.
     https://oncodaily.com/oncolibrary/breast-oncology/breast-cancer-nccn-v4

  Pharmacy Times — 'NCCN Guidelines on CDK4/6 Inhibitor Treatment Regimens' (June 2026)
     got right     : Explicitly stated that Category 1 designation is 'based off whether or not overall survival has been demonstrated' and correctly identified this as the 'delineating' factor between ribociclib and the other two agents; acknowledged all three drugs are listed in first-line guidelines.
     still omitted : Does not engage with the CI-overlap argument, the secondary-endpoint status of OS in these trials, or the comparative evidence from NMA and real-world studies.
     https://www.pharmacytimes.com/view/nccn-guidelines-on-cdk4-6-inhibitor-treatment-regimens

  ScienceDirect / meta-analysis (2025) — 'Risk of QTc prolongation and major cardiovascular adverse events associated with CDK4/6 inhibitors'
     got right     : Correctly identified that ribociclib specifically warrants routine ECG surveillance for QTc prolongation; found VTE risk was highest with abemaciclib (RR 5.14); explicitly called for 'drug-tailored rather than class-wide cardiovascular monitoring'; confirmed ribociclib's QTc signal is not shared by abemaciclib or palbociclib at the same level.
     still omitted : Does not connect the drug-specific monitoring burdens to the guideline grading question. Does not note that abemaciclib's VTE risk represents a countervailing monitoring burden that the draft's framing ('the one documented difference') understates.
     https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305737225001914

  [NEW · SERIOUS] the draft's claim about the coverage is contradicted
     quote         : “The one documented difference between the drugs runs the other way from the grade. Ribociclib alone requires cardiac monitoring — electrocardiograms before treatment and during it. That is a burden, not a benefit.”
     but           : The Kappel et al. 2024 NMA (Scientific Reports) found that treatment discontinuation and death due to adverse events were significantly higher with abemaciclib than with palbociclib and ribociclib, and that abemaciclib carried significantly more grade 3–4 diarrhea and higher GI toxicity. The 2025 cardiovascular meta-analysis (ScienceDirect) found abemaciclib carries a VTE risk of RR 5.14 (95% CI 3.09–8.54), requiring its own intensified monitoring. These are additional documented differences between the drugs — not captured by the draft's claim that cardiac monitoring is 'the one documented difference.'
     see           : https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53151-8
     fix           : Revise to: 'The most clinically salient documented difference that runs counter to the grade is ribociclib's requirement for cardiac monitoring. But the drugs differ in other ways too: abemaciclib carries significantly higher VTE risk and higher rates of treatment discontinuation due to adverse events than either comparator in network meta-analysis — differences that cut against abemaciclib rather than ribociclib, and that the grade does not reflect either.'

  [NEW · SERIOUS] the draft's claim about the coverage is contradicted
     quote         : “Two studies have compared all three anyway, by different methods on different data — a network meta-analysis of seven randomised trials, and a large real-world weighted comparison. Neither is a randomised head-to-head trial and neither should be read as one. They agree with each other: no separation between the three.”
     but           : The PALMARES-2 real-world study (Annals of Oncology, published ~August 2026, reported by Targeted Oncology) found that abemaciclib and ribociclib were associated with significantly longer real-world PFS compared with palbociclib (abemaciclib vs. palbociclib aHR 0.76 [0.63–0.92]; ribociclib vs. palbociclib aHR 0.83 [0.73–0.95]) in endocrine-sensitive HR+/HER2− advanced breast cancer. This is a third real-world comparative study that does find separation — specifically on PFS, not OS — and it does not agree with the draft's summary of 'no separation between the three.'
     see           : https://www.targetedonc.com/view/real-world-study-shows-differences-in-efficacy-among-cdk4-6-inhibitors-for-advanced-breast-cancer
     fix           : Revise to specify that the two studies cited agree on OS specifically, and acknowledge that at least one additional real-world study (PALMARES-2) finds PFS separation favoring abemaciclib and ribociclib over palbociclib — which is consistent with the draft's separate point that palbociclib's result is a different case.

  [NEW · MINOR] the draft's claim about the coverage is contradicted
     quote         : “Abemaciclib's hazard ratio for death is 0.80, on an interval running 0.64 to 1.02.”
     but           : The published final OS result from MONARCH 3 (Annals of Oncology, Goetz et al. 2024) is HR 0.804, 95% CI 0.637–1.015. The draft rounds the lower bound to 0.64 (correct) but rounds the upper bound to 1.02 (the actual figure is 1.015, which rounds to 1.02 — acceptable). However, the point estimate of 0.80 is a rounding of 0.804. These are minor rounding differences but a careful reader comparing to the source will notice the CI is 0.637–1.015, not 0.64–1.02.
     see           : https://www.annalsofoncology.org/article/S0923-7534(24)00139-X/fulltext
     fix           : Report as HR 0.804 (95% CI 0.637–1.015) to match the published figure exactly, or note that figures are rounded to two decimal places throughout.

  What this piece can add that careful coverage does not:
     No existing coverage — including the NMA and real-world studies — quantifies the degree of CI overlap between the MONARCH 3 and MONALEESA-2 OS intervals or frames the grading question as one about what a threshold-crossing on a secondary endpoint tells us about the drug versus the trial's statistical power. The draft's specific calculation that 97% of ribociclib's CI lies inside abemaciclib's CI is original and not present in any source found; this is the layer beneath even the most careful comparative coverage, and it is the piece's strongest contribution.

